<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 3: AI and the Illusion of Autonomy  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/case-study-plan.md):
  - Case study: Amazon "Just Walk Out" (contested reporting, Amazon disputes it).
  - Podcast: episode 54, which contrasts it with M-Pesa ("phygital").
  - Not used here, kept for later chapters: the Facebook moderators' case in
    Kenya (Chapter 8, Who is Responsible?). Chapter 2 already has the Sama /
    OpenAI story.
  - The practice question and Try It are still to do.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-illusion-of-autonomy" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>AI and the Illusion of Autonomy</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-illusion-of-autonomy">
        <p>
            Imagine walking into a shop, picking up what you want, and walking
            out again.
            There's no till, no queue and no card to tap.
            The receipt turns up on your phone.
            When Amazon opened its first shop like this in Seattle in January
            2018, the New York Times called it a store of the future
            (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            The idea was that cameras and AI would work out what you'd picked
            up, and charge your account.
            No people needed.
            Then, in 2024, reports said that wasn't the whole story.
        </p>

        <p>
            This chapter is about the gap between how AI looks and how it
            works.
            A lot of systems look as though they run by themselves.
            Behind many of them there are people, labelling data, checking
            answers and fixing mistakes, often a long way away, and often
            where nobody can see them.
            We saw one version of that in the last chapter.
            Here we look at another, and ask what it means for a system to be
            called <q>autonomous</q> at all.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain what <q>autonomy</q> means for an AI system, and
                        why it's often misunderstood.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe how human labour is used to train, monitor and
                        correct AI tools, and give examples of humans in the
                        loop.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Reflect on the social, emotional and economic
                        consequences of hidden human labour.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Weigh up the ethics of AI systems that look autonomous
                        but rely on unequal, and sometimes exploitative, human
                        work.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-illusion-of-autonomy-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Amazon's Just Walk Out</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-shop">
            <title>The shop of the future</title>

            <p>
                Amazon opened its first Amazon Go shop to the public in Seattle
                on 22 January 2018
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
                The idea was simple.
                Cameras would register what you picked up, and charge it to
                your account as you left
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
                Wikipedia describes it as a mix of computer vision, deep
                learning and sensors, with cameras in the ceiling and weight
                sensors on the shelves
                (<xref ref="biblio-amazon-go-wikipedia" text="custom">Wikipedia, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The technology was called Just Walk Out.
                In 2020, Amazon began offering it to other retailers, and in March 2021 it opened its first Just Walk Out shop in
                London, an Amazon Fresh
                (<xref ref="biblio-amazon-go-wikipedia" text="custom">Wikipedia, n.d.</xref>).
                For shoppers, the attraction was obvious, because there were no
                queues.
                For Amazon, it was a showpiece for AI.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-reports">
            <title>What the reports said</title>

            <p>
                In April 2024, Theo Wayt, a reporter at The Information,
                reported that Just Walk Out relied on a lot of human help.
                According to the reporting, a team of about 1,000 workers in
                India reviewed camera footage to confirm what people had bought
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                He also reported that in 2022, about 700 out of every 1,000
                sales were reviewed by people.
                Some of those reviews were only because someone had picked an
                item up and put it back.
                Even so, Wayt said there were far more reviews than Amazon
                would have liked
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                There was a clue for customers.
                Sometimes the receipt arrived hours after you'd left the shop.
                That delay came from a reviewer double-checking the camera
                footage
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-dispute">
            <title>Amazon's reply</title>

            <p>
                Amazon disputed this.
                It called the claims <q>misleading and inaccurate</q>.
                Its position is that human verification happened after the
                initial point of sale in the shop, and that the workers were
                only training the AI so it would become more accurate
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The radio station KUOW, which reported on this, summed it up as
                somewhere in the middle.
                Wayt's view was that Just Walk Out was an advanced technology
                that still needed a lot of people in India to supply the
                training data, so that it could recognise what you were picking
                up
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Notice what both sides agree on.
                People were doing work for this system, and it wasn't
                <q>no people needed</q>.
                What they disagree about is how much of that work was happening
                as you shopped, and how much was training for later.
                We don't know how much, and we haven't found any account of
                what these workers were paid, or what their working conditions
                were like.
                So we can't say anything about that here.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-why">
            <title>Why hide the people?</title>

            <p>
                KUOW notes that paying cheaper labour elsewhere to do menial
                tasks, like training AI or moderating content, is common in the
                tech industry, especially in start-ups that rely on showy
                technology to win investors.
                Benjamin Shestakofsky, a sociologist at the University of
                Pennsylvania who studies how start-ups work, told KUOW that
                the work is usually outsourced to countries where wages are
                lower
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                His point is that hiding the human labour can make a company
                look more valuable, because investors think they're getting
                powerful AI with low costs.
                It also makes the work cheaper.
                If nobody knows the workers are there, it's easier to get away
                with paying them as little as possible
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
                His comments were about the industry in general, and not about
                Amazon in particular.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-next">
            <title>What happened next</title>

            <p>
                Amazon confirmed that it was laying off US staff who worked on
                installing Just Walk Out in new shops, and on supporting it
                (<xref ref="biblio-kuow-2024" text="custom">Denkmann and Cowan, 2024</xref>).
                It also announced plans to replace it with Dash Carts, which
                are smart shopping carts, in its Amazon Fresh shops
                (<xref ref="biblio-amazon-go-wikipedia" text="custom">Wikipedia, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Then, on 27 January 2026, Amazon announced that it was closing
                its physical Amazon Go and Amazon Fresh shops.
                It said it hadn't yet created a distinctive customer
                experience with the right economics to grow at scale.
                Just Walk Out carries on in other companies' premises, like
                concession stands at sports stadiums
                (<xref ref="biblio-axios-2026" text="custom">Axios, 2026</xref>;
                <xref ref="biblio-techcrunch-amazon-2026" text="custom">TechCrunch, 2026</xref>).
                We haven't seen anything that says the closures were because of
                the human review.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <p>
                Whatever the number, both sides agree that people were involved, so
                the story that no people were needed wasn't quite true.
                People were part of the system.
                They checked it, corrected it and trained it.
                That's the human in the loop from Chapter 1.
                What changes from system to system is whether you can see them.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    <q>Autonomous</q> is a claim, and a claim you can test.
                    When something is sold as automatic, ask who's checking it,
                    where they are, and what they're paid.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                It also connects back to the last chapter.
                The Kenyan workers who made ChatGPT safer, and the people who
                reviewed shoppers' footage, were doing very different jobs.
                But both were work that the finished product didn't mention.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Podcast                                                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-illusion-of-autonomy-podcast">
        <title>Hidden Jobs and Visible Jobs</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                The ideas in this section come from a conversation between me
                and David Stern, from the IDEMS Podcast
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-054" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 54, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about half an hour long.
                It starts with Amazon's shops, and ends up somewhere else, which
                is how most of our conversations go.
                It's also available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/054-beyond-the-hype-the-reality-of-ai-powered-shops/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-illusion-of-autonomy-podcast">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 54: Beyond the Hype, The Reality of AI-Powered Shops</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-illusion-of-autonomy-podcast"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/3836/054-beyond-the-hype-the-reality-of-ai-powered-shops.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-indian-shops">
            <title>Indian-powered shops</title>

            <p>
                David's first reaction when I mentioned the story was to call
                them Indian-powered shops.
                His understanding is that although there was a bit of AI
                involved, it was mostly about moving the cashiers somewhere
                else, to a processing unit where people watched videos in
                India.
                That has a lot less glitz than <q>AI-powered</q>, and as I
                said, they weren't going to put it on the poster.
                (This is his understanding, and as we've seen, Amazon disputes
                how much of it is true.)
            </p>

            <p>
                David's wider point is that almost all AI relies on a huge,
                hidden human workforce, who do the coding and help the AI to learn.
                That's what we saw with the Kenyan workers in the last
                chapter.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-phygital">
            <title>Phygital: M-Pesa</title>

            <p>
                David's example of technology doing it differently is M-Pesa.
                Remember, M-Pesa is the mobile money service from Kenya.
                His argument is that in a high-resource environment, people
                think of technology as a way to remove humans.
                In low-resource environments, powerful technology tends to
                create jobs.
            </p>

            <p>
                His version of the M-Pesa story goes like this.
                People in cities sent money home to relatives in rural areas,
                and many carried it back on buses.
                That was dangerous, and buses were hijacked because they were
                carrying so much cash.
                Some people found a different way.
                They bought a phone for their family in the village, and sent
                credit for it.
                The family could then charge neighbours to use the phone, so the
                credit became money.
            </p>

            <p>
                Someone noticed this and formalised it.
                You could take your phone to an M-Pesa agent, pay a tiny fee,
                and put money into your phone, or take it out again.
                Pesa is the Swahili word for money, and M is for mobile.
                David says anywhere you go across Africa now, the shops you see
                most are M-Pesa shops, where people make a living from offering
                these services.
                (This is David's account, and we haven't checked it against
                other sources.)
            </p>

            <p>
                David uses the word <term>phygital</term> for this, meaning
                physical as well as digital.
                A phygital technology builds people into the system in a way
                that you can see.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    A good test for a technology is whether it creates more
                    human interaction and visible jobs than it removes, or
                    whether it moves them out of sight.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                By that test, M-Pesa is a good example, and, if the reports are
                right, a shop where the cashier's job has been moved to a video
                review team in another country isn't.
                David gives a smaller example.
                He had a two-minute chat with a cashier about a lot of
                anchovies, and it left him feeling happy.
                He argues that a good phygital idea creates more human
                interaction than it removes.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-ethics">
            <title>Is it ethical?</title>

            <p>
                David is clear about his view.
                He thinks employing people to watch videos in India, because
                it's cheaper than employing cashiers in San Francisco or
                London, is unethical.
                He thinks those cashier jobs should be created where the shops
                are.
            </p>

            <p>
                But he doesn't want to be too hard on Amazon.
                His reasoning is that they were trying to push the boundaries of
                technology, found out that the technology wasn't where they
                thought it was, and then stopped.
                As he puts it, it's not their fault that AI has made promises it
                couldn't yet keep.
            </p>

            <p>
                I asked David what the incentive is for a company to keep
                people, when people are expensive and software, once it's
                built, isn't.
                His answer is that good business isn't about maximising
                short-term profit, and that if a company offers good service
                and is still profitable, it can win out over time.
                He adds that he doesn't think it will play out well in the short
                term, because short-term profit is seen as more important than
                long-term market share.
            </p>

            <p>
                He's also confident that over a few hundred years we'll be able to
                build technology that can do what Amazon was trying to do.
                But he says that the tools we have now are very powerful, and that
                we don't want a society where humans don't interact.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-illusion-of-autonomy-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A lot of AI that looks autonomous depends on people who
                        label data, check answers and fix mistakes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Amazon's Just Walk Out was reported to rely heavily on
                        reviewers in India.
                        Amazon disputes how much.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Hiding human work can make a company look more
                        valuable, and makes the work easier to underpay.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A phygital technology, like M-Pesa, builds visible jobs
                        and human contact into the system.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <q>Autonomous</q> is a claim to test, and the questions
                        to ask are who's checking, where they are, and what
                        they're paid.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

