    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-illusion-of-autonomy">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-assignment">
            <title>Assignment: the human labour behind AI</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose one of two cases: Amazon's Just Walk Out, from this chapter, or
                    the Kenyan data workers who helped to make ChatGPT safer, from Chapter 2.
                    In no more than 500 words, respond to these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Briefly summarise what you learned about the human labour behind
                            that AI system.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What surprised or disturbed you most?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What does this case reveal about the limits of AI autonomy?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Do you think the company was acting responsibly? Why or why not?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would a more ethical and transparent system look like?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The Facebook moderators come up again in Chapter 8, where we ask who is
                    responsible for them.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-1">
            <title>The illusion of autonomy</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    AI often looks automated and intelligent, but many systems
                    rely heavily on human labour to label data, review offensive
                    content or correct errors.
                    How does that change what you think AI can and can't do?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-2">
            <title>Human in the loop</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In your own words, what is a human in the loop?
                    Give one example from this chapter, and one from the last
                    chapter.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-3">
            <title>Human in the loop, at what cost?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Were you aware that AI systems rely on this kind of
                            human labour?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Should companies be more open about it?
                            Why, or why not?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you want a company to tell you about the
                            people behind a product that's called <q>AI</q>?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-4">
            <title>Whose account do you trust?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The reporting said about a thousand workers in India
                    reviewed Just Walk Out sales.
                    Amazon says they were mostly training the AI.
                    What evidence would you want to see to decide which is
                    closer to the truth, and who would have it?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-5">
            <title>Power and inequality</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Many of the workers behind AI, in this chapter and the last
                    one, are in countries with lower incomes than the companies
                    they work for.
                    How do power and global inequality show up in the way AI is
                    built?
                    What does this say about who benefits from AI, and who pays
                    the price?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-6">
            <title>Visible and hidden jobs</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Think of a technology where you live that has created jobs
                    you can see, like M-Pesa agents, and one that has moved work
                    out of sight.
                    What's the difference between them, and who decided?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-illusion-of-autonomy-7">
            <title>An honest label</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Imagine you're writing the label for a product that's
                    advertised as <q>AI-powered</q>, but which relies on people
                    checking its work.
                    Write the label you'd want to see.
                    What would you include, and what would the company probably
                    want to leave out?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

